. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1 * 
. 
.         * We run a linear mixed effects model with random intercepts at the i
> ndividual (pid) and household (cid) levels.
.         * We predict the support for state-involvement to relieve care work (
> used to operationalize attitude towards gender
.         * equality measures) from whether the person has daughters (1 = yes, 
> 0 = everything else), controlling for a year-
.         * fixed effect. 
. 
.         
.         * mixed support_state hasdaughters syear || cid: || pid:, reml
.         
.         * Because the model does not converge in the full data set, we change
>  the estimation approach. We instead use a linear
.         * panel regression (GLS) with random effects at the level of particip
> ants (pid). 
.         * We predict the support for state-involvement to relieve care work (
> used to operationalize attitude towards gender
.         * equality measures) from whether the person has daughters (1 = yes, 
> 0 = everything else), controlling for a year-
.         * fixed effect. 
. 
.         xtset pid

Panel variable: pid (unbalanced)

.         xtreg support_state hasdaughters syear, re  

